Z||000009||JSocha||09-28-2001||05:57 PM||||Just do what I would do in this case and use an Omni Directional Impact Device and you'll be able to make it and anything else fit in your current cigerette lighter not to mention any other.  <P>I'm sure you have one lying around in your toolbox.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>There also great when you get P'd off and during repairs and need to hit it a few times.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000010||theogre||09-28-2001||07:52 PM||||There are basically two types of lighter socket used by GM Ford and Chrysler. The diameter of all of them is the same but there are 2 diferant depths. As far as I can remember... only one company actually makes the lighters used by all three...<P>If you socket really is smaller diameter then someone has to have changed it somewhere along the line. The best fixt would be to repalce it with the correct hardware. (Don't worry about the light... ) You can get suitable parts from almost any parts store in north america. You're looking at maybe $10.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
